; ==============================================================================
; 5. HELP, IDE INSTALLATION & BUILT-IN TOOLS
; ==============================================================================

[TipsHelpAndTools]

; --- 'Help and Tools' window interface ---
ToolsWindowTitle = PBBrowser Help and Tools
TipsListTitle    = Tips
HelpListTitle    = Help topics
ToolsListTitle   = Tools
Shortcut         = Shortcut
DontShowInMenu   = Do not show in menu
Execute          = Execute
AboutTHATTitle   = About 'Help and Tools'
AboutTHATEx      = This window contains tips for using PBBrowser, as well as help on its main functions.%newline%The %quote%Tools%quote% list offers specialized functions, some of which may still be in beta version.%newline%The %quote%Shortcut%quote% button allows you to assign a keyboard shortcut to the tools you want to use more easily, enabling you to launch them directly from the main window of PBBrowser.%newline%%newline%Click on a line in one of the lists on the left to get more explanations.

; --- 'Tips' topics ---
PBShortCutsTitle  = PBBrowser keyboard shortcuts
PBShortCutsEx     = As long as the PBBrowser window is active, you can use the following keyboard shortcuts:%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%'Escape' key: Closes PBBrowser.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%'Return' key: Equivalent to clicking 'Search' (or 'Look Up').%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%'CTRL' + 'Q' keys: If an expression is selected on screen (especially in the %quote%Details%quote% or %quote%Found in...%quote% panel), immediately launches a search on that expression, even if it is not underlined (double-click on the expression, then press 'CTRL' + 'Q'). If no text is selected, focuses the 'Expression' input field.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%'CTRL' + 'V' keys: Pastes the clipboard content into the 'Expression' field and immediately launches the search.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%'CTRL' + 'I' keys: Opens the 'Help and Tools' window.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%'CTRL' + 'F' keys: Opens a local search dialog inside the currently displayed panel.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%'CTRL' + '+', 'CTRL' + '-', and 'CTRL' + '0' keys: Zoom in, zoom out, or reset the text zoom level in the active panel.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%'CTRL' + 'S' and 'CTRL' + 'R' keys: Save the content of the active panel in TXT or RTF format.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%'TAB' and 'SHIFT' + 'TAB' keys: Cycle through the various panels in the lower half of the window. This is handy for reviewing the different element lists.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%'Up', 'Down', 'PageUp', and 'PageDown' keys: Scroll the active panel in the lower half of the window.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%'<-' and '->' keys: Navigate backward and forward through your search history in the 'Details' and 'Found in...' panels (equivalent to the arrow buttons at the top of both panels). In other panels, these keys act like 'TAB' and 'SHIFT' + 'TAB'.
SearchLimitsTitle = Search capabilities
SearchLimitsEx    = Since version 2.0 of PBBrowser, search capabilities have been significantly expanded. It is now possible to search for complex expressions (%quote%MyVar = 10%quote%, for example) even if they span across multiple lines (which the PureBasic IDE search does not allow). Furthermore, searches for complex expressions are insensitive to the number of spaces present in the code or in the searched expression: a search for %quote%MyVar = 10%quote% will find %quote%MyVar     = 10%quote%, for example.%newline%This new search function automatically detects whether the searched expression is a complex expression (several words, for example) or the name of an element (a procedure or a variable, for example). In cases where it concerns a local variable name used in several procedures or a procedure name used in several modules, it is now possible to restrict the results to a particular procedure or a particular module. If the searched expression corresponds to multiple elements (homonymous structure and variable, for example), it is also possible to choose the type of element for which you want to display the results.%newline%Finally, the %quote%+%quote% button allows you to refine the search mode to extend it to the contents of comments or strings present in the code. This button also provides access to a Find/Replace function across your entire code (including Include and XInclude files), even if this code has not been grouped into a project file (.pbp).
CreateToolsTitle  = Create your own tools in PBBrowser.
CreateToolsEx     = Since PBBrowser is designed for programmers, it wouldn't be surprising if you wanted to dive into its code, which is freely downloadable. But with more than two hundred procedures to analyze, the task might seem daunting, even though its code is abundantly commented (in English only, unfortunately for non-English-speaking programmers, though translation tools can easily help).%newline%If you're satisfied with its current functionality and only want to add features by taking advantage of some of its existing procedures, you can add functions without touching the existing ones:%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%1- Execute the %quote%Retrieve the source code of PBBrowser%quote% command found in the list at the bottom left of this window.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%2- Open the %quote%PBB_CustomTools.pb%quote% file in %quote%PBBrowser resources/Source Codes%quote%. You will find all the necessary explanations to integrate one of your procedures into the %quote%Tools%quote% list located at the bottom left of this window.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%3- Recompile the application with PureBasic.

; --- 'Help' topics ---
AboutPBBrowserTitle = General presentation
AboutPBBrowserEx    = file:IntroPBBrowser.rtf
MainFileTitle       = Main file and included files
MainFileEx          = PBBrowser is primarily designed to be called from the PureBasic editor. When this happens, PBBrowser will naturally examine the code of the file from which the call originated, but it will also extend its examination to all files it considers 'related' to this file:%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%If the user has opened a main file containing 'IncludeFile' or 'XIncludeFile' directives, the included files will be added to the list of files to be examined.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%If the examined file has been configured in the PureBasic editor via the 'Compiler Options' menu and designated as linked to a %quote%Main file%quote%, then this %quote%Main file%quote% becomes PBBrowser's Main file, and all files it declares via 'IncludeFile' or 'XIncludeFile' are added to the list of files to be examined.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%In summary, the list of files will be completed either upstream or downstream of the examined file, based on the indications PBBrowser can extract.
ChangeMainFileTitle = Change main file
ChangeMainFileEx    = The 'Change' button on the main window allows you to change the main file. When you do this, PBBrowser clears all the information it had gathered from its previous examination and starts a complete exploration of the files identified as being related to the new main file.%newline%However, if the %quote%new%quote% file you choose was already in the previous list of files, the operation will produce no changes.
SearchLaunchTitle   = Launch a search in PBBrowser
SearchLaunchEx      = There are four ways to launch a search in the code of your files:%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%1- From the PureBasic editor, if PBBrowser has been installed in the editor's tools, simply click or double-click on any word, then go to the %quote%Tools / PB Browser%quote% menu, or press the keyboard shortcut for PBBrowser (CTRL + Q, if you kept the default shortcut). This will automatically launch PBBrowser if it is not already open, and a search for the chosen word will be immediately performed. If PBBrowser is already running, the search will be performed directly.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%2- Once PBBrowser is running and a main file has been designated, you can enter an expression in the 'Expression' field, then click 'Search' or press the 'Return' key.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%3- When you click on an underlined expression, a search is immediately launched with that expression.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%4- If you want to search for an expression that appears in one of PBBrowser's panels but is not underlined, double-click on the expression, then press CTRL + Q on the keyboard.
SearchModTitle      = PBBrowser search modes
SearchModEx         = PBBrowser searches inside your files' code in two complementary ways:%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%1- The %quote%Found in...%quote% panel displays the results of a direct search in the code. The expression is searched for in exact compilation order, and the surrounding code is explored to determine the context in which the expression appears (inside a module, procedure, or macro, for example). This search mode displays results for a given expression almost immediately.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%2- As soon as PBBrowser starts, a systematic exploration of the files linked to the main file begins in order to compile an exhaustive list of all the 'elements' that make up the code (procedures, macros, structures, constants, variables, modules, imports, etc.). This very fast exploration gives an overview of the project and determines which elements are actually used by the main file: it examines all code outside procedures and macros, marks any elements referenced there as 'used', and propagates that status across the entire call graph. Names of unused elements are displayed in gray across all panels.
NavigationTitle     = Navigation in PBBrowser
NavigationEx        = When you click on an underlined expression in one of PBBrowser's panels, it can produce several effects depending on the context:%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%From the %quote%Files%quote% panel, a click will open the chosen file in the PureBasic editor.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%From the %quote%Lists%quote% panel, a left-click on an element's name will open that element in the %quote%Details%quote% panel. A right-click will open it in the %quote%Found in...%quote% panel.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%From the %quote%Details%quote% panel, clicking on a filename will open the file in the PureBasic editor; a left-click on an element's name will open that element in the %quote%Details%quote% panel; a right-click will open it in the %quote%Found in...%quote% panel; clicking on a PureBasic keyword (such as 'If' or 'EndIf') or on the name of a native function (such as 'FindString' or 'MessageRequester') will directly open the corresponding topic in the PureBasic help file (or launch an online search if the help file is unavailable).%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%From the %quote%Found in...%quote% panel, clicking on a line number in square brackets will position the PureBasic editor on the corresponding line; clicking on a filename will open the file in the PureBasic editor; a left-click on an element's name will open that element in the %quote%Details%quote% panel; a right-click will open it in the %quote%Found in...%quote% panel.%newline%%newline%Also remember that the 'Tab' key or 'SHIFT' + 'TAB' allows you to switch between panels, and the '<-' and '->' keys allow you, depending on the panel, to move through your page history or switch panels.


[AddToolToPB]

; --- Keyboard shortcut capture ---
CaptureShortCutsTitle    = Keyboard shortcut
CaptureShortCutsTitleFor = Keyboard shortcut for
PBCaptureTitle           = PBBrowser keyboard shortcut
UsedShortCutsTitle       = List of already used keyboard shortcuts:
AT_Explanations          = By pressing keyboard keys, choose the shortcut you want to assign to the command:
PBSC_Explanations        = By pressing keyboard keys, choose the shortcut that will launch PBBrowser%newline%from the PureBasic editor:
IsAllreadyUsed           = is already used
AT_DontInstall           = Do not install

; --- Native install / uninstall tools ---
InstallPBBrowserTitle   = Install PBBrowser in PureBasic tools
InstallPBBrowserEx      = Clicking 'Execute' will install PBBrowser in the 'Tools' menu of your PureBasic editor.%newline%If PBBrowser is already listed there, this command lets you update its executable path or change its keyboard shortcut.%newline%Remember that the PureBasic editor must be restarted for the changes to take effect.%newline%%newline%If this automatic installation fails, you can install PBBrowser manually in PureBasic's tools:%newline%Create a new custom tool in the PureBasic editor and configure it as follows:%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Commandline: select the compiled version of PBBrowser (PBBrowser.exe) on your disk%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Arguments: %quote%%HOME%quote% %quote%%WORD%quote% %quote%%SELECTION%quote% %quote%%CURSOR%quote% %quote%%FILE%quote% %quote%%TEMPFILE%quote%%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Name: PB Browser%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Event to trigger the tool: Menu Or Shortcut%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Shortcut: CTRL + Q%newline%%newline%If you want to configure PBBrowser as a portable application, insert %quote%\PORTABLE %quote% before the %quote%%HOME%quote% parameter.
UninstallPBBrowserTitle = Uninstall PBBrowser from PureBasic tools
UninstallPBBrowserEx    = Clicking 'Execute' will uninstall PBBrowser from your PureBasic editor's tools.%newline%This command does not delete PBBrowser from your disk; you will need to remove its files manually if desired.%newline%Remember that the PureBasic editor must be restarted for the changes to take effect.

; --- Installation result and error messages ---
ToolInstalledSuccessfully     = PBBrowser was successfully installed in the tools of the editor.%newline%You will need to close the editor and restart it to complete the installation.
ToolRemovedSuccessfully       = PBBrowser was removed from the editor's tools.%newline%You will need to close the editor and restart it to complete the removal.
ToolAllreadyInstalled         = PBBrowser is already installed in the tools.%newline%If you want to change its shortcut, go to the 'Tools' menu in the PureBasic editor, or uninstall it and reinstall it.%newline%Remember that the PureBasic editor must be restarted for the changes to take effect.
ToolNotInstalled              = PBBrowser was not found in the tools list.%newline%Remember that the PureBasic editor must be restarted for the changes to take effect.
UnableToFindPBBrowser         = PBBrowser.exe was not found in your folders.%newline%If you have already created an executable ('.exe') version of this application, you will need to indicate its location.%newline%The executable must exist to install it in the tools.
ShowPBBrowserPath             = Designate the location of 'PBBrowser.exe'.
ToolExeNotFound               = The executable version of PBBrowser (PBBrowser.exe) was not found in your folders.%newline%The executable must exist to install it in the tools.
UnableToFindPBPref            = The PureBasic preferences file could not be found.
UnableToOpenPBPref            = The PureBasic preferences file could not be read.
UnableToCreateToolsConfigFile = The PureBasic tools configuration file could not be created.
UnableToReadToolsConfigFile   = The PureBasic tools configuration file could not be read.
UnableToBackUpToolsConfigFile = The PureBasic tools configuration file could not be backed up.
UnableToModifyToolsConfigFile = The PureBasic tools configuration file could not be modified.


[BuiltInTools]

; --- Tool 1: Cosmetic Enhancer (PBB_CustomTools.pb) ---
CosmeticFileTransformerTitle = Cosmetic Enhancer
CosmeticFileTransformerEx    = Reformats all project files in a single blazing-fast pass to standardize code spacing and readability.%newline%The tool automatically normalizes spaces around mathematical, logical, and comparison operators (+, -, *, /, <, >, =, &, |...), commas, parentheses, statement colons (':'), and PureBasic keywords, while strictly protecting comments, string literals (both standard %quote%...%quote% and escaped ~%quote%...%quote%), character literals, inline assembly ('!'), module scope operators ('::'), pointers ('*Ptr'), and labels.%newline%%newline%Example of transformation:%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Mid (CodeContent\s,dTest,PosInCode-dTest)%newline%  becomes:%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Mid(CodeContent\s, dTest, PosInCode - dTest)%newline%%newline%A backup copy of your project in a separate folder is offered before any modification is applied.

; --- Tool 2: Create or Update a PureBasic Project (.pbp) (PBB_CustomTools.pb) ---
CreateUpdateProjectTitle = Create or update a PureBasic 'project'
CreateUpdateProjectEx    = Just as you can do manually via the 'Project' menu in the PureBasic editor, this function creates or synchronizes a PureBasic project file (.pbp) from the inclusion tree built by PBBrowser. PureBasic projects allow you in particular to:%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Maintain a complete list of source files and binary assets required by your program;%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Enable IDE auto-completion for procedures, structures, variables, and constants declared across your included files;%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Centralize compiler options, icons, and version information;%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Load in one go the code files relevant to a specific development stage.%newline%%newline%If the project already exists, PBBrowser will offer to add any newly detected files ('IncludeFile', 'XIncludeFile', and 'IncludeBinary') as well as remove any obsolete files no longer referenced anywhere in the code.%newline%%newline%If no project exists yet, the tool will create one automatically using the main file's compiler options.
DefaultTarget            = Default target
NoProjectFound           = No project was found for the examined code.%newline%What would you like to do?
LookForProjectManually   = Select the project manually
CreateNewProject         = Create a new project
ProjectAllreadyOpen      = This project is currently open in PureBasic and cannot be modified.%newline%Close it if you wish to examine and update it, then click OK.
WouldYouAdd              = The following files are listed in your program's code but are not present in the project.%newline%Do you want to add them to the project?
WouldYouDel              = The following files are present in the project but are not referenced anywhere in your program's code.%newline%Do you want to remove them from the project?
FilesToDelete            = Files that could be removed from the project:
ProjectNotModified       = The project was up to date. It has not been modified.
WouldYouOpenProject      = Would you like to open the project in the PureBasic editor?

; --- Tool 3: Retrieve PBBrowser Source Code (PBB_CustomTools.pb) ---
ExportSourceCodeTitle = Retrieve the source code of PBBrowser
ExportSourceCodeEx    = Since PBBrowser is designed for programmers, it would not be surprising if you wanted to dive into its code, which is open-source and freely downloadable. Click %quote%Execute%quote% to open the download webpage.

; --- Tool 4: Erase Comments from the Code (PBB_CustomTools.pb) ---
EraseCommentsTitle  = Erase comments from the code
EraseCommentsEx     = Opens a dialog allowing you to strip all source files in the project (main file and included files) in a single pass to reduce code size or prepare a clean distribution.%newline%You can choose to erase all comments (while preserving string literals and PureBasic's trailing IDE compiler options block) and, optionally, also remove indentation, extra spaces, and blank lines.%newline%For safety, working on a copy of the project in a new folder is enabled by default so your original files remain untouched.
EC_EraseAllComments = Erase all comments from the code
EC_EraseExtraSpaces = Erase extra spaces and empty lines from the code
EC_WorkOnCopy       = Work on a copy of the project in folder:

; --- Tool 5: Generate Procedure Declarations (PBB_SignatureTool.pb) ---
SignatureToolTitle   = Generate Procedure Declarations
SignatureToolEx      = Generates in a single click a list of procedure declarations ('Declare') or signatures ('Procedure') for a specific file or the entire project, ordered either in exact compilation order or alphabetically. Your original source files are never modified.%newline%%newline%Depending on the selected options, this tool addresses three needs:%newline%%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Produce a ready-to-include .pb header file: instantly solves declaration-order and mutual-recursion issues across procedures (while cleanly handling public 'DeclareModule' exports versus private module procedures).%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Document project architecture and call graphs: by keeping leading header comments ('; Role:...') and enabling analysis annotations, each signature is enriched with its called and calling procedures (%quote%who calls whom?%quote%), referenced Global, Threaded, and Shared variables, as well as any unprotected or unused local variables.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Share a lightweight blueprint with a collaborator or an AI: copy the complete architectural skeleton and function contracts of a large codebase to the clipboard without cluttering the context with procedure bodies.
ST_SecScope          = Scope and Ordering
ST_TargetFiles       = Target files:
ST_AllProjectFiles   = All project files
ST_SortSource        = Source order (grouped by file)
ST_SortAlpha         = Alphabetical order
ST_ExcludeModules    = Exclude procedures inside Modules
ST_SecFormat         = Signature and Comments
ST_UseDeclare        = Use 'Declare' keyword
ST_KeepProcedure     = Keep 'Procedure' keyword
ST_KeepHeaderCom     = Keep header comments below signature (; Role:...)
ST_KeepStandaloneCom = Keep standalone comment blocks outside procedures
ST_SecMeta           = Analysis Annotations (inserted as ';' comments)
ST_AnnotateCalls     = List called procedures and calling procedures
ST_AnnotateGlobals   = List Global, Threaded, and Shared variables used
ST_AnnotateVarIssues = Report Unprotected and Unused local variables
ST_SecOutput         = Output Destination
ST_CopyClipboard     = Copy generated list to clipboard
ST_SaveToFile        = Save generated list to file:
ST_OpenAfterSave     = Open file after saving
ST_FileFilter        = PureBasic (*.pb, *.pbi)|*.pb;*.pbi|Text (*.txt)|*.txt|All files (*.*)|*.*
ST_DoneClipboard     = The declarations list has been copied to the clipboard.
ST_DoneSaved         = The declarations list has been saved to: %1
ST_DoneBoth          = The declarations list has been copied to the clipboard and saved to: %1

; --- Tool 6: Check Procedure Declarations (PBB_SignatureTool.pb) ---
ProcVariablesTitle = Check procedure declarations
ProcVariablesEx    = Audits the body of every procedure in the main file and its included files (accounting for macro expansions and module isolation) to safeguard and clean up local declarations:%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Unprotected variables: whenever an implicit local variable is neither declared as 'Global', 'Threaded', or 'Shared', nor as 'Protected' or 'Static', the tool automatically inserts a 'Protected' line at the top of the procedure (right below any header comments, preserving file indentation and line endings). This makes your code 'EnableExplicit'-ready and prevents accidental collisions with global variables of the same name.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Unused variables: whenever a formal parameter or declared local variable is never referenced in the procedure, the tool inserts a comment flagging it so you can easily clean up dead remnants.%newline%%newline%A backup copy of your project is offered before any modification, followed by a summary report of all updated procedures.

; --- Tool 7: Export Standalone Single-File .pb (PBB_CodeFlattener.pb) ---
FlattenToolTitle   = Export Standalone Single-File .pb
FlattenToolEx      = Turns any PureBasic program in a single click — from a simple script to a large project split across dozens of IncludeFile directives and external .pbi libraries — into a single, fully standalone .pb file, assembled in the exact order the compiler reads it and immediately ready to compile. Your original files are never modified.%newline%%newline%With its customizable cleanup options, this tool addresses five needs:%newline%%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Clean up a project (even a single-file one!) of all its dead code in one pass: no need to have included files to take advantage of it. Used on a single .pb file, the tool surgically strips all unused procedures, macros, structures, interfaces, constants, Global/Define variables, and Declare statements, instantly delivering a clean, streamlined version of your source.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Easily share code on forums or with a collaborator: no more incomplete archives, missing XIncludeFile paths on your recipient's machine, or hitting message size limits on the PureBasic forum! By checking the removal of unused elements, inactive CompilerIf zones, comments, or extra spaces, you keep only the essential core actually executed by your program (while automatically adjusting IncludeBinary and icon paths).%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Archive a %quote%frozen snapshot%quote% of a complex project and its libraries: when your projects share common .pbi files that evolve over the months, a library update can break an older program. Exporting your project into a single file freezes the exact state of all its code at a given point in time: an ideal, ultra-convenient backup where a simple Ctrl+F lets you find and retrieve an older version of any procedure!%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Isolate a bug for a test report: extract in seconds a minimal, self-contained, reproducible version of a complex project without exposing or bundling the rest of your personal libraries.%newline%•%nonbreakingspace%Feed your code to an AI: copy the streamlined output straight to the clipboard to give an AI assistant the entire useful context of your program in one block, without saturating its memory with unused code.
FT_SecContent      = Code Content and Cleanup
FT_AddFileBanners  = Insert file-transition comment banners between merged segments
FT_PruneDeadZones  = Strip inactive CompilerIf / CompilerSelect zones
FT_PruneUnused     = Strip unused procedures, structures, macros, constants, and globals
FT_EraseComments   = Erase all code comments
FT_CleanSpaces     = Compact inline spaces and strip blank lines
FT_RewriteBinPaths = Rewrite IncludeBinary and UseIcon paths relative to target file
FT_SecOutput       = Output Destination
FT_OpenInIDE       = Open generated file in the PureBasic IDE
FT_FileFilter      = PureBasic Source (*.pb)|*.pb|All Files (*.*)|*.*


